> > > > Anki is the big one, but like Lindar I also liked the multiple
> > > > choice aspect of smart.fm, which others do not have.
> > >
> >
> > Is it possible to do multiple-choice in Anki?
>
> No:
> http://ankisrs.net/docs/FrequentlyAskedQuestions.html#_can_i_do_multiple_choice_questions
>

Drat.

> is there anything available now that is similar we could use?
>
> I am not aware of anything else that is multiple-choice based.
>

Double-drat.


> > > > It might be worth us (and others) working on a new one; it
> > > > could hardly be written any *worse* than smart.fm was.
> > > > Someone has started a proto-community around this at
> > > > http://twitter.com/openlanglearn
> >
> > By this, do you mean the Smart.fm code, or the Lojban lessons? I'm very
> much
> > hoping you mean the code, btw. :)
>
> Neither; the idea of making a free smart.fm clone.  We don't have
> the smart.fm code, nor any right to it.
>

You said "worse than smart.fm". I was asking if you meant that we could
hardly write a /program/ worse than the Smart.fm one, or that we could
hardly write a /lesson/ worse than the Smart.fm one. (And by program, as an
example: The Mozilla Firefox team obviously does not have access to the M$
Internet Explorer source code, however, Firefox is (arguably) a much better
program than IE is.(It is definitely better in terms of OS portability, for
instance.))


